# macOS tablet input and embedded cursor

## Photoshop compatibility candidate — 2026-09-10

Branch `macos-pen-capabilities`, based on main after the interface-MTU merge.
User reports pressure in Pixelmator Pro but none in Photoshop. This is not
proof of a transport failure or of Photoshop compatibility in earlier tests.

The first candidate (1.0.91) corrects two proximity metadata fields only:
- SDK27 IOLLEvent.h defines mask0x447: deviceID, absoluteX/Y, buttons, pressure.
  Advertise precisely these supplied fields, never tilt/rotation/vendor fields.
- pointerID is the tool index, not the point/proximity matching deviceID.
  Use index0 for our single tool; retain matching deviceID1 throughout.

Keep pressure values, geometry, pen/eraser sequencing, event posting, teardown,
authorization, Linux input and Client/transport code unchanged. Do not spoof
a Wacom vendor/model/serial. Do not inject repeated proximity events or add
app-specific detection speculatively. First test Photoshop with a pressure brush,
then Pixelmator, application switching while hovering, tip/side buttons, eraser,
reconnect, and 1:1/scaled coordinates. If only application switching fails,
capture delivery evidence before adding a narrowly scoped proximity refresh.

Automated tests inspect both CGEvent fields and converted AppKit NSEvents,
including 21 pressure levels at three geometries, tool transitions and denied
delivery cleanup. These are construction tests, not Photoshop acceptance.

References (contracts, not copied implementations):
- https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsevent/capabilitymask
- https://developer-docs.wacom.com/docs/icbt/macos/ns-events/ns-events-basics/
- https://www.wacomeng.com/mac/Developers%20Guide.htm (pointerID/deviceID and capabilities)

## Previous accepted baseline

Branch: `macos-tablet` (Client `plank/macos-tablet`), based on main80acb10.
Merged and pushed September 9, 2026: root main `c6015b3`, Client main `de18b22d`.
Existing candidate artifacts retain their original branch-qualified version;
this merge did not rebuild or redeploy them.
Keep Linux raw-HID/normalized-pen behavior unchanged. Candidate1.0.79 is now
installed on the dedicated Mac after the user disconnected; Client install is manual.

Implementation checkpoint: native mapper and schema2 launch negotiation are now
implemented for candidate1.0.79-macos-tablet. Shared type7 payload/common-c and
transport archive are unchanged. Mac mapper tests pass775 checks, keyboard/mouse
suite2567 checks, real QUIC/auth-input suite200 checks/eight scenarios. Side
buttons and tip multi-click are retained, with per-event revocation rollback.
Clean package builds passed from rootcf40262/Clientde18b22d. Both artifacts are
in `artifacts/packages/`. User reports working pressure in Pixelmator and accepts
the candidate, explicitly authorizing commit, push and merge. The browser tester
still reports 0/50% (browser unspecified); browser pressure is not qualified.
A separate Linux live regression run and detailed button/cursor matrix were not
performed; do not infer those results from the overall user acceptance.

1. Fix embedded-session cursor visibility. SDL3.4.2's Wayland ShowCursor
   implementation uses `mouse->cur_cursor` for tablet tools even when passed
   NULL to hide. Use one transparent SDL cursor for hidden embedded-session
   local pointers, restoring the default image for local UI and teardown.
   Do not create another Wayland connection or change the local-cursor contract.
2. Qualify public Quartz pressure/proximity events before advertising Mac pen
   support. The standalone probe first inspects native/AppKit conversion without
   posting; live mode uses only its owned window in the same consented desktop.
   A synthetic pass is not arbitrary-app acceptance.
3. Add normalized pen handling to the existing authenticated Mac input owner,
   mapping normalized coordinates through current point/pixel geometry. Correct
   hover/down/drag/up/proximity/cancel and tool identity; finite bounded pressure.
   Reuse the current type7 packet, never forward raw USB or claim Wacom hardware.
   Reject malformed data; release only owned state on stop, never into a new user.
4. Negotiate pen support only after actual Mac delivery passes. Verify the Client
   selects one source, with no duplicate mouse synthesis. Preserve local toolbar,
   letterbox and timeout pointer behavior; test layout changes and reconnects.
5. Build on the designated Mac/linux-client-builder; user installs End-User Client.
   Record real pressure-sensitive application acceptance and Linux regression
   coverage separately. User authorized merging after Pixelmator acceptance;
   Linux live checks remain unperformed. No Tablet Margins/ExpressKeys/ring
   support in this scope.

No fixed30s audio startup delay was found in the earlier read-only inspection;
the user deferred further alert-startup investigation. Do not modify that path.

## Qualification checkpoint — 2026-09-09

- Client transparent-cursor unit passes on linux-client-builder with SDL3.4.2 headers:
  100 hide/show cycles, one allocation, failure handling, teardown restoration,
  unused helper makes no SDL calls. Full Client compilation and package gates pass;
  user native-app acceptance is recorded above, without a separate cursor matrix.
- SDK27 warnings-as-errors pressure probe passes six construction inspections.
  Signed own-process Aqua delivery passes all six events (mask63): proximity
  enter/exit, down(.25), drag(.75), drag(.50), up(0). No permission changes,
  global event tap, keyboard input, other-app clicks or Host replacement.
- An immediate burst delivered mask59, omitting the first intermediate drag.
  Disabling AppKit mouse coalescing did not change that. The final bounded test
  spaces samples250ms apart and targets only its own PID; all values arrive.
  This establishes public event delivery, not real Wacom/application acceptance
  or high-rate sampling behavior. The production injector uses the session event
  path, which still needs qualification; this test does not prove equivalence.
- Build with `scripts/build-macos-tablet-pressure.sh`, using the retained signing
  identity. `--inspect` posts nothing. Install the signed Probe only on the
  dedicated Mac and run `run-graphical-probe.sh gui/UID ... --tablet-pressure`.
  Normal launch has no test mode. Runner refuses LoginWindow and cleans its job.
- The temporary Probe application was moved out of Applications after testing;
  no previous Probe was installed. Host stayed1.0.78 during those probes. The
  subsequent native mapper/negotiation implementation is now candidate1.0.79.
  User subsequently accepted native pressure in Pixelmator. Browser pressure
  remains an optional follow-up, not a reason to modify the accepted path.
